\end{align}
where $\tilde{\Psi}_4^{l,m,\infty}(f)$ is the Fourier component of $\Psi_4^{l,m,\infty}(t)$ and $f_\text{cut}$ is set to be $0.8m\Omega_0/(2\pi)$.

In general, gravitational waves for each $(l,m)$ mode are decomposed into the amplitude and phase as
\begin{align}
  h^{l,m,\infty}(t_\text{ret}) = A^{l,m,\infty}(t_\text{ret}) e^{-i\Phi^{l,m}(t_\text{ret})}, \label{eq:freqGW}
\end{align}
and instantaneous gravitational-wave frequency is defined by $d\Phi^{l,m}/dt_\text{ret}$.
