We employ several steps to determine the analytic model's parameters and the model contains several frequency components, which is why it is not straightforward to find the optimal values describing the data. We find that by introducing a {\it normalization factor} $\mathcal{N}$ we obtain better fits. We thus define
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{Fplusfit}
h_{+}^\mathrm{Fit}(t) &=& \mathcal{N} \cdot {h_\mathrm{+}}(t),
\end{eqnarray}
with ${h_\mathrm{+}}(t)$ given as in  Eq.~\eqref{analytic model equations}. We note that when simpler (under-performing) analytic models are employed (consisting of only one or two frequency components) the normalization factor $\mathcal{N}$ is dropped. We stress that the normalization factor is only introduced as part of our procedure for determining the best fit - with other fitting procedures it may not be required.

To summarize, the complete analytic model of the $+$ polarization of the signal amplitude reads
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{analytic model equations-2}
h_{+}^\mathrm{Fit}(t) &=& \mathcal{N}\cdot\Big(h_{+}^\mathrm{peak}(t)\cdot \mathcal{W}(t; s)  +\sum_\mathrm{i} h_{+}^\mathrm{i}(t)\Big),\\
&\mbox{for}&\ i=\mbox{spiral, }2\pm0, \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
